.SH TYPES
.SS Tokenizer
.IP
.EX
pub enum Tokenizer { Structure, Legacy }
.EE
.PP
Selects the tokenizer variant.
\f[B]Structure\f[R] (default) is the modern tokenizer that understands
\f[B]key=value\f[R] pairs, quoted strings, and JSON contexts.
\f[B]Legacy\f[R] is the original whitespace\-oriented tokenizer.
.PP
Call \f[B]Tokenizer::tokenize(line)\f[R] to produce a
\f[B]Vec<Token>\f[R], or use the free function \f[B]tokenize(line)\f[R]
as shorthand for \f[B]Tokenizer::Structure\f[R].
.SS Token / TokenType
.IP
.EX
pub struct Token { pub ty: TokenType, pub text: String }

pub enum TokenType {
    Literal   = 0,  // static word/identifier
    Delimiter = 1,  // whitespace or punctuation
    Timestamp = 2,  // recognized date/time
    Ipv4      = 3,  // dotted\-quad IPv4
    Quoted    = 4,  // quoted string contents
    Number    = 5,  // numeric value
    Json      = 6,  // nested JSON value
    JsonKey   = 7,  // key in key=value or {\[dq]key\[dq]:...}
}
.EE
.PP
\f[B]TokenType::is_structural()\f[R] returns \f[B]true\f[R] for Literal,
JsonKey, and Json; \f[B]TokenType::is_parameter()\f[R] for Number,
Timestamp, Ipv4, and Quoted.
.PP
The detector decides per token, with \f[B]Token::is_structural()\f[R]
and \f[B]Token::is_parameter()\f[R]: these follow the type, except that
a Literal containing a digit or a \f[B]/\f[R] (\f[I]web01\f[R], an id, a
path or URL) is a parameter.
.SS structure_signature
.IP
.EX
pub fn structure_signature(tokens: &[Token]) \-> StructureHash
.EE
.PP
Computes the FNV\-1a 64\-bit hash of the structural tokens (type + text
for each) and of a placeholder for each parameter, in order, with a
separator byte.
Two lines with the same structure but different dynamic values produce
the same \f[B]StructureHash\f[R]; moving a parameter (\f[I]foo 1
bar\f[R] vs \f[I]foo bar 1\f[R]) changes it.
.SS structure_string
.IP
.EX
pub fn structure_string(tokens: &[Token]) \-> String
.EE
.PP
Renders the signature as a space\-joined string, with \f[B]<*>\f[R] for
each parameter.
This is the human\-readable form of the signature (the \f[CR]static\f[R]
printer output).
.SS Detector
.IP
.EX
pub struct Detector { /* ... */ }

impl Detector {
    pub fn new() \-> Self;
    pub fn with_config(config: DetectorConfig) \-> Self;
    pub fn with_store(config: DetectorConfig, store: ProfileStore) \-> Self;
    pub fn observe(&mut self, system: &str, line: &str, now: i64)
        \-> Option<Anomaly>;
    pub fn observe_tokens(&mut self, system: &str, line: &str,
        tokens: &[Token], now: i64) \-> Option<Anomaly>;
    pub fn sweep(&mut self, now: i64) \-> Vec<Anomaly>;
    pub fn config(&self) \-> &DetectorConfig;
    pub fn clock(&self) \-> Option<i64>;
    pub fn store(&self) \-> &ProfileStore;
}

pub struct Anomaly {
    pub system: String,
    pub kind: AnomalyKind,
    pub hash: Option<StructureHash>,  // None for SystemAway
    pub signature: String,
    pub line: String,                 // empty for EventStop / SystemAway
    pub score: f64,
}
.EE
.PP
\f[I]now\f[R] is unix seconds of event time; see
\f[B]time::event_time\f[R] to take it from the line itself.
.PP
\f[B]observe\f[R] tokenizes \f[I]line\f[R], looks up or creates the
per\-system profile, updates statistics, and returns
\f[B]Some(Anomaly)\f[R] if the event is anomalous.
Returns \f[B]None\f[R] if the event matches learned\-normal behavior.
.PP
\f[B]observe_tokens\f[R] is identical but accepts pre\-computed tokens
(useful when the caller has already tokenized the line).
.PP
\f[B]sweep\f[R] reports, as of \f[I]now\f[R], periodic structures that
have been silent for more than \f[B]stop_factor\f[R] mean intervals
(\f[B]EventStop\f[R]) and systems silent for more than
\f[B]away_secs\f[R] (\f[B]SystemAway\f[R]).
Call it periodically (e.g.\ once per minute of event time).
Each silence is reported once, until the structure or system sends a
newer line; the output is sorted by system.
A structure is periodic once it has at least three intervals whose
standard deviation is under half their mean.
Each system is judged on its own clock: \f[I]now\f[R] minus how far
behind the latest time its last line was when it arrived, so a system
whose timestamps lag the others\[cq] (local time, shipping delay) is not
reported for the lag.
.PP
\f[B]clock\f[R] returns the latest time passed to \f[B]observe\f[R] or
\f[B]sweep\f[R] (for a detector made with \f[B]with_store\f[R], the
latest time in the store).
.PP
\f[B]with_store\f[R] resumes from a previously learned
\f[B]ProfileStore\f[R]; with the \f[B]serde\f[R] feature,
\f[B]ProfileStore\f[R] implements \f[B]Serialize\f[R] and
\f[B]Deserialize\f[R].
